            "body": "> I just saw that in neovim 0.12 the `nvim_open_win()` is now populated with statusline and I assume we use that (haven't look the code yet)\r\n\r\nNot quite. This is a result of the fact that in Neovim>=0.12 [floating windows now are able to show statusline](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b346b87fb25779f2d9803fae0e378595611f79fb/runtime/doc/news-0.12.txt#L111-L112). Previously it was impossible.\r\n\r\nThe problem lies in the way how active/inactive statusline is dynamically set: it sets statusline value after the 'mini.files' explorer is opened. Which it reasonably assumes now that the user wants to show statusline in that window.\r\n\r\nTo accommodate for that, I'd recommend going the 'mini.statusline' route and set one global value of 'statusline' option which inside itself dynamically chooses which statusline (active or inactive) to show. So something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n_G.stl_active = function()\r\n  return table.concat({\r\n    '%#StatusLine#',\r\n    branch_status(),\r\n    '%y',\r\n    '%=',\r\n    '[%f]',\r\n    '%=',\r\n    '%h%w%m%r[%l:%c][%P]',\r\n    get_time(),\r\n  })\r\nend\r\n\r\n_G.stl_inactive = function() return '%#StatusLineNC#[%f]' end\r\n\r\nvim.o.statusline =\r\n  '%{%(nvim_get_current_win()==#g:actual_curwin || &laststatus==3) ? v:lua.stl_active() : v:lua.stl_inactive()%}'\r\n```",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I just saw that in neovim 0.12 the <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim_open_win()</code> is now populated with statusline and I assume we use that (haven't look the code yet)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Not quite. This is a result of the fact that in Neovim&gt;=0.12 <a href=\"https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b346b87fb25779f2d9803fae0e378595611f79fb/runtime/doc/news-0.12.txt#L111-L112\">floating windows now are able to show statusline</a>. Previously it was impossible.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The problem lies in the way how active/inactive statusline is dynamically set: it sets statusline value after the 'mini.files' explorer is opened. Which it reasonably assumes now that the user wants to show statusline in that window.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">To accommodate for that, I'd recommend going the 'mini.statusline' route and set one global value of 'statusline' option which inside itself dynamically chooses which statusline (active or inactive) to show. So something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"_G.stl_active = function()\n  return table.concat({\n    '%#StatusLine#',\n    branch_status(),\n    '%y',\n    '%=',\n    '[%f]',\n    '%=',\n    '%h%w%m%r[%l:%c][%P]',\n    get_time(),\n  })\nend\n\n_G.stl_inactive = function() return '%#StatusLineNC#[%f]' end\n\nvim.o.statusline =\n  '%{%(nvim_get_current_win()==#g:actual_curwin || &amp;laststatus==3) ? v:lua.stl_active() : v:lua.stl_inactive()%}'\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-c1\">_G</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">stl_active</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>()\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">table.concat</span>({\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%#StatusLine#<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-c1\">branch_status</span>(),\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%y<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%=<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>[%f]<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%=<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%h%w%m%r[%l:%c][%P]<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>,\n    <span class=\"pl-c1\">get_time</span>(),\n  })\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">_G</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">stl_inactive</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%#StatusLineNC#[%f]<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">o</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">statusline</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>%{%(nvim_get_current_win()==#g:actual_curwin || &amp;laststatus==3) ? v:lua.stl_active() : v:lua.stl_inactive()%}<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span></pre></div>",
            "bodyText": "I just saw that in neovim 0.12 the nvim_open_win() is now populated with statusline and I assume we use that (haven't look the code yet)\n\nNot quite. This is a result of the fact that in Neovim>=0.12 floating windows now are able to show statusline. Previously it was impossible.\nThe problem lies in the way how active/inactive statusline is dynamically set: it sets statusline value after the 'mini.files' explorer is opened. Which it reasonably assumes now that the user wants to show statusline in that window.\nTo accommodate for that, I'd recommend going the 'mini.statusline' route and set one global value of 'statusline' option which inside itself dynamically chooses which statusline (active or inactive) to show. So something like this:\n_G.stl_active = function()\n  return table.concat({\n    '%#StatusLine#',\n    branch_status(),\n    '%y',\n    '%=',\n    '[%f]',\n    '%=',\n    '%h%w%m%r[%l:%c][%P]',\n    get_time(),\n  })\nend\n\n_G.stl_inactive = function() return '%#StatusLineNC#[%f]' end\n\nvim.o.statusline =\n  '%{%(nvim_get_current_win()==#g:actual_curwin || &laststatus==3) ? v:lua.stl_active() : v:lua.stl_inactive()%}'",
